.TH KISSCHK 1
.SH NAME
kisschk \- check the kiss device, and the stack above it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kisschk frame
.br
.B kisschk ax25
.br
.B kisschk far
.RI [ n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Kisschk
makes a
.IR pipe (2),
gives one end to
.IR kiss (3)
and keeps the other, and speaks KISS on it by hand \- assembling and
reading the bytes from the specification rather than from
.B devkiss.c
\- so the device is checked against something that shares none of its
code, and the whole stack above it runs in one kernel with no radio
fitted.
.PP
.B Frame
checks the framing: that a payload containing both FEND and FESC
survives being escaped in each direction, that the
.B iounit
is what
.I kiss (3)
says, and that a frame which is not a data frame is dropped rather
than delivered.
.PP
.B Ax25
attaches
.I ax25 (3)
to the device, sends one UI frame, and decodes what comes out of the
pipe: the destination and source callsigns out of the address field,
the control byte, the PID and the information.  That is what a TNC
would be handed.
.PP
.B Far
holds the far end and prints what arrives, so another program \-
.IR aprs (1),
say \- can send through the stack while this reads it.  Run it with
.BR & .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
	; kisschk ax25
	ax25-iounit ok
	destination ok
	source ok
	control-is-ui ok
	pid-is-no-layer-3 ok
	KISS GB1SAT-3>APZINF:>over a pipe
.EE
.PP
and with
.IR aprs (1):
.PP
.EX
	; kisschk far 3 &
	; aprs setup GB1SAT-3 '#k/kiss0/data'
	; aprs status in-orbit
	KISS-FAR GB1SAT-3>APZINF:>in-orbit
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/kisschk.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.I kiss (3),
.I ax25 (3),
.I aprs (1)
.SH BUGS
The pipe is made by this program and goes away when it exits, so
.B far
must outlive whatever is sending through the device.
